{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to the Eticas AI Risk Taxonomy v3.0.0, which frameworks provide direct coverage for Agentic AI, and what structural gap does this reveal?", "answer": "Only purpose-built frameworks like AIUC-1, CSA Agentic Profile, OWASP, and IBM Atlas provide direct coverage for Agentic AI, revealing a structural governance gap as major compliance frameworks predate agentic AI.", "evidence": [{"page": 17, "quote": "Only the purpose-built frameworks (AIUC-1, the CSA Agentic Profile, OWASP) and the IBM Atlas provide direct coverage, and all date from 2025 or later."}, {"page": 19, "quote": "Every compliance-tier framework providing binding obligations was finalized before or as agentic AI emerged, and most reference-tier frameworks predate it too."}]},
  {"question": "How does the Eticas taxonomy distinguish between technical explanation and stakeholder communication in its Transparency and Explainability category?", "answer": "It separates technical explanation (interpretability, rationale, feature importance) from stakeholder communication (user disclosure, transparency), as they serve different functions and methods.", "evidence": [{"page": 16, "quote": "The category separates technical explanation from stakeholder communication because they serve different functions with different methods."}]},
  {"question": "What is the main organizational structure of the Eticas AI Risk Taxonomy, and how does it differ from the IBM AI Risk Atlas?", "answer": "Eticas uses risk-domain organization, aligning with audit workflow, while IBM Atlas organizes by lifecycle stage, aligning with development workflow.", "evidence": [{"page": 18, "quote": "Risk-domain organization (Eticas, MIT V4, NIST 600-1, AIR 2024) organizes by what can go wrong and aligns with audit workflow. Lifecycle-stage organization (IBM Atlas, NIST RMF functions) organizes by where in the pipeline risk surfaces and aligns with development workflow."}]},
  {"question": "Which IEEE standards are mapped at category level in the Eticas taxonomy, and what domains do they cover?", "answer": "IEEE Std 7001-2021 (transparency), 7002-2022 (data privacy), 7003-2024 (algorithmic bias), and 2894-2024 (explainable AI) are mapped at category level on their relevant domains.", "evidence": [{"page": 22, "quote": "The taxonomy also maps four IEEE standards: the conformityassessable IEEE Std 7001-2021 (transparency of autonomous systems), 7002-2022 (data privacy process), and 7003-2024 (algorithmic bias considerations), placed in the compliance tier, and the advisory IEEE Std 2894-2024 (architectural framework for explainable AI), placed in the reference tier. Each is mapped at category level on its relevant domain."}]},
  {"question": "Which subcategory in the Eticas taxonomy addresses the risk of confidential or proprietary information exposure, and what earlier concept did it absorb?", "answer": "The subcategory 'sensitive-information-leakage' addresses this risk, absorbing the earlier 'confidential-information-leakage' concept.", "evidence": [{"page": 18, "quote": "the exposure of confidential or proprietary information through model outputs, is captured in the taxonomy by the established subcategory sensitive-information-leakage, which absorbed the earlier confidential-information-leakage concept and covers confidential and proprietary data alongside personal data, framed as a confidentiality risk rather than a new intellectual-property category; copyright and patent questions remain outside what a system audit can measure."}]},
  {"question": "What is the main limitation of the MIT AI Risk Repository V4 for operational audit use, as described in the Eticas taxonomy paper?", "answer": "Its comprehensiveness leads to heterogeneity and cognitive overload, making it a map of the territory rather than a tool for operational audit use.", "evidence": [{"page": 20, "quote": "Its comprehensiveness, however, limits operational use. It preserves the heterogeneity of its sources rather than normalizing to one vocabulary; equivalent concepts from different frameworks appear as separate entries. It mixes abstraction levels, placing mechanisms (\"dataset bias\"), observable harms (\"discriminatory outcomes\"), and organizational root causes (\"lack of diversity in development teams\") as coordinate entries. And at many hundreds of entries it exceeds the cognitive load of audit scoping. None of this is a flaw in a meta-review; it is the cost of comprehensiveness. But it means the Repository is a map of the territory, not a tool for traversing it. It does not, and does not aim to, show how any one risk is measured."}]}
]}
